nd function-call arguments, which the chat `choices` shape never exposed, so those replies were skipped entirely before. Request-side extraction adds tool-call and legacy function_call names and arguments from chat `messages` and Responses `input` function_call items, since `iter_message_text` reads message content only and an injection payload carried in a tool call would otherwise reach the model uninspected. It also covers the model-visible request fields that live outside `messages` and `input` entirely: the legacy Completions `prompt`, the Responses-API top-level `instructions` and the request's tool definitions (name, description and the `parameters` JSON schema), so a payload cannot slip past Akamai by moving out of message content into a field the model still sees. Anthropic `/v1/messages` gets special handling because hook-based guardrails receive the provider-native body rather than the translated one; when the call type is `(a)anthropic_messages` the request is run through the shared `LiteLLMAnthropicMessagesAdapter` first, which lifts the top-level `system` prompt into a system message, `tool_use` and `tool_result` content blocks into `tool_calls` and tool messages and tool `input_schema` into `tools[].function.parameters`, so the same extraction inspects them; a translation failure falls back to raw extraction so text content is still scanned. The Anthropic response is inspected symmetrically: a non-streaming `/v1/messages` reply arrives as a native Anthropic message dict, so its `content` text and `tool_use` inputs are walked directly, and a streamed reply arrives as raw Anthropic SSE bytes, so the shared passthrough assembler rebuilds them into a `ModelResponse` that the existing output extraction reads, which means streamed Anthropic text and tool calls are buffered and withheld before delivery when a rule blocks. Output extraction also pulls the reasoning a model returns outside `content`, since `get_content_from_model_response` reads only ordinary content and tool calls: chat `message.reasoning_content` and `message.thinking_blocks[].thinking`, and Responses-API `output[].summary[].text`. Because `stream_chunk_builder` keeps `reasoning_content` and `thinking_blocks` on the assembled response, the chat-streaming and Anthropic-streaming paths that already route through the assembled `ModelResponse` are covered as well; encrypted `redacted_thinking` blocks carry no readable text and are skipped. Streaming responses are covered by a new `async_post_call_streaming_iterator_hook` that collects the chunks, assembles them and runs the same detect request, then either replays the buffered chunks when allowed or emits an SSE error event when Akamai blocks; the previous behavior only ran the post-call success hook through the deferred stream path, which is audit-only and fires after the content has already been streamed to the client. Chat streams are assembled with `stream_chunk_builder`; Responses-API streams are inspected from the terminal `response.completed` event, which carries the full `ResponsesAPIResponse` ## QA runbook 1. Set `AKAMAI_FIREWALL_API_KEY`, `AKAMAI_FIREWALL_CONFIGURATION_ID` and `AKAMAI_FIREWALL_USER_APPLICATION_ID` in your environment 2. Start the proxy with a config that defines an `akamai_firewall_for_ai` guardrail with `mode: [pre_call, post_call]` and `default_on: true` 3. Send the two curl requests above and confirm the benign one returns 200 and the injection one returns 400 with an "Blocked by Akamai Firewall for AI" detail 4. Repeat the injection request with `"stream": true` and confirm the SSE response carries an `error` event with the Akamai block rather than the model tokens, proving the response is withheld before delivery 5. Send a request that forces a tool call whose arguments carry the disallowed content and confirm it is blocked on post_call, proving tool-call arguments are inspected and not just plain content 6. Send the same injection payload to `/v1/responses` (both non-streaming and with `"stream": true`) and confirm it is blocked, proving the Responses API surface is inspected 7. Send a follow-up request whose prior assistant turn carries the injection payload inside `tool_calls[].function.arguments` and confirm it is blocked on pre_call, proving inbound tool-call arguments are inspected 8. Send requests that carry the disallowed content only in the legacy Completions `prompt`, only in the Responses-API top-level `instructions`, and only in a tool definition's `description`, and confirm each is blocked on pre_call, proving model-visible fields outside `messages` and `input` are inspected 9. Send Anthropic `/v1/messages` requests that carry the disallowed content only in the top-level `system`, only inside an assistant `tool_use` block, and only in a tool `input_schema`, and confirm each is blocked on pre_call, proving the provider-native body is translated and fully inspected 10. Send an Anthropic `/v1/messages` request whose output carries the disallowed content (both non-streaming and with `"stream": true`) and confirm each is blocked on post_call, proving the native Anthropic response dict and the streamed Anthropic SSE are inspected and the stream is withheld before delivery 11. Enable extended thinking and send a prompt whose visible answer is benign but whose reasoning must contain the disallowed content, confirm it is blocked while the same prompt with thinking off is allowed, proving `reasoning_content` and `thinking_blocks` are inspected on both the non-streaming and streaming paths 12. Open the Admin UI guardrails page and confirm the provider dropdown logo, config fields and Guardrail Garden card ### Final Attestation - [x] The tests check the right things, including the edge cases, and regressions in the respective real-world customer use-cases are not possible after this PR

## TLDR Problem this solves: - No native cap on usage per arbitrary caller-supplied tag identity - Existing tag budgets are dollars-only, reset on a schedule not a window - Existing tpm/rpm limits scope to key/user/team, never to a tag value - No native cap on concurrent in-flight requests per tag identity How it solves it: - A model group declares token/request/dollar/concurrency limits under `model_info.tag_rate_limits` - Enforced on every routing attempt via `async_filter_deployments`, so fallback hops are checked too - Bucketed by `epoch_second // period_seconds`, covering both daily caps and RPM-style caps - Requests/concurrency admission is atomic check-and-increment; tokens/dollars account on success Squashed from the 18 rate-limiting commits on #36459 and rebased onto `litellm_internal_staging`. Docs PR: BerriAI/litellm-docs#851 ## Relevant issues Fixes #35116 ## Linear ticket ## Pre-Submission checklist - [x] I have added meaningful tests - [x] My PR passes all CI/CD checks (e.g., lint, format, unit tests) - [x] My PR's scope is as isolated as possible; it only solves 1 specific problem - [ ] I have received a Greptile **Confidence Score of at least 4/5** before requesting a maintainer review (Greptile reviews automatically once the PR is opened; only comment `@greptileai` to re-request a review after pushing changes) ## Delays in PR merge? If you're seeing a delay in your PR being merged, ping the LiteLLM Team on [Slack (#pr-review)](https://join.slack.com/t/litellmossslack/shared_invite/zt-3o7nkuyfr-p_kbNJj8taRfXGgQI1~YyA). ## Screenshots / Proof of Fix Verified against a live local proxy (`proxy_cli.py --config ... --detailed_debug`) at commit `4206cb1e9a386b828f50d7b5cd000e1ab1c7b5c4`, real Anthropic API calls to `claude-haiku-4-5` (no mocks), `router_settings.num_retries: 0` so a rejection surfaces directly instead of being masked by an automatic retry. Config used, a chain with a 2-requests-per-60-seconds limit and a 1-concurrent-request limit, both keyed on `end_user_id`: ```yaml model_list: - model_name: haiku-tagged litellm_params: model: anthropic/claude-haiku-4-5 api_key: os.environ/ANTHROPIC_API_KEY model_info: tag_rate_limits: request_limits: limits: - name: per_minute tag_id: end_user_id limit: 2 period_seconds: 60 concurrency_limits: limits: - name: inflight tag_id: end_user_id limit: 1 period_seconds: 300 litellm_settings: callbacks: ["tag_rate_limiter"] router_settings: num_retries: 0 ``` Two truly parallel requests from the same tag; exactly one succeeds and the other gets a 429 attributing the rejection to the concurrency limit specifically: ``` $ curl -s -o /tmp/r1.json -w "%{http_code}
" http://localhost:4003/v1/chat/completions \ -H "Authorization: Bearer $KEY" -H "X-Litellm-Tags: end_user_id:fix-check-2" \ -d '{"model":"haiku-tagged","messages":[{"role":"user","content":"reply with exactly one word: pineapple"}]}' & $ curl -s -o /tmp/r2.json -w "%{http_code}
" http://localhost:4003/v1/chat/completions \ -H "Authorization: Bearer $KEY" -H "X-Litellm-Tags: end_user_id:fix-check-2" \ -d '{"model":"haiku-tagged","messages":[{"role":"user","content":"reply with exactly one word: pineapple"}]}' & wait 200 429 $ cat /tmp/r2.json {"error":{"message":"tag_rate_limit_exceeded","type":"throttling_error","provider_specific_fields":{"error":"tag_rate_limit_exceeded","type":"concurrency","tag_id":"end_user_id","tag_value":"fix-check-2","limit_name":"inflight","limit":1.0,"period_seconds":300}}} ``` Sequential 2-requests-per-60-seconds behavior: 2 requests from the same tag succeed, the 3rd gets a 429 naming the tag identity and offending limit. A different tag value succeeds while the first is exhausted, confirming per-identity isolation, and a request with no `X-Litellm-Tags` header at all also succeeds, confirming an unresolved entry fails open: ``` $ for i in 1 2 3; do curl -s -w "
%{http_code}
" http://localhost:4003/v1/chat/completions \ -H "Authorization: Bearer $KEY" -H "X-Litellm-Tags: end_user_id:rpm-check" \ -d '{"model":"haiku-tagged","messages":[{"role":"user","content":"reply with exactly one word: pineapple"}]}'; done 200 200 429 {"error":{"message":"tag_rate_limit_exceeded", ... "type":"requests","tag_value":"rpm-check","limit_name":"per_minute","limit":2.0,"period_seconds":60}} $ curl ... -H "X-Litellm-Tags: end_user_id:other-tag" ... 200 $ curl ... (no X-Litellm-Tags header) 200 ``` ## Type 🆕 New Feature ## Changes - `litellm/proxy/hooks/tag_rate_limiter.py`: new opt-in `CustomLogger`. Builds a `model_info.tag_rate_limits`-driven limits index per model group, resolves tag identity from `metadata.tags`, enforces via `async_filter_deployments` on every routing attempt. Requests/concurrency admission is an atomic, all-or-nothing check-and-increment; tokens/dollars stay read-then-account-on-success in `async_log_success_event`. Concurrency slots release on success or failure with a TTL as the safety net for a leaked reservation. - `litellm/types/router.py`: new `TagRateLimitEntry` / `TagRateLimitGroup` / `TagRateLimits` models and a `ModelInfo.tag_rate_limits` field. - `litellm/litellm_core_utils/litellm_logging.py`, `litellm/__init__.py`: register `tag_rate_limiter` as a resolvable opt-in callback string, mirroring the existing `dynamic_rate_limiter_v3` registration. - `ui/litellm-dashboard/src/lib/http/schema.d.ts`: regenerated for the new `ModelInfo.tag_rate_limits` field. - `tests/test_litellm/proxy/hooks/test_tag_rate_limiter.py`: unit tests for tag identity extraction, chain-wide vs per-deployment limit resolution, enforcement, per-entry fail-open, accounting, the atomic requests race fix, concurrency reserve/release on success and failure, and the index TTL refresh for a length-preserving deployment update. ### Final Attestation - [ ] The tests check the right things, including the edge cases, and regressions in the respective real-world customer use-cases are not possible after this PR

## TLDR Problem this solves: - Freeform (custom) tool calls log no output to Langfuse - Every code-mode turn looks like the model answered nothing How it solves it: - Map `custom_tool_call` output items into the Langfuse output payload ## User Flow Before: a developer proxying a coding agent that uses a freeform `exec` tool sees blank outputs in Langfuse, so a whole session reads as if the model never answered 1. They send `POST https://litellm-domain/v1/responses` with `"tools": [{"type": "custom", "name": "exec", ...}]` 2. The response comes back with a `custom_tool_call` item carrying the JavaScript to run, and the agent runs it 3. They open that generation in Langfuse: **Output is `null`**, while input, token counts and cost are all correct 4. Every turn where the model called `exec` looks empty, so the trace can't be used to review what the agent actually did After: the same request records the tool call it actually made 1. They send the same `POST https://litellm-domain/v1/responses` with the same custom tool 2. The response comes back with the same `custom_tool_call` item, and the agent runs it 3. They open that generation in Langfuse: **Output shows the call** — `[{"id": "...", "name": "exec", "call_id": "call_...", "type": "custom_tool_call", "input": "console.log(6*7)
"}]` 4. Turns that called `exec` are reviewable alongside the plain-text and `function_call` turns that already worked ## Relevant issues Fixes #37430 ## Linear ticket ## Pre-Submission checklist - [x] I have added meaningful tests - [ ] My PR passes all CI/CD checks (e.g., lint, format, unit tests) — `lint` is green; the only red check is `misc / Run tests`, failing on `tests/test_litellm/batches/test_batch_utils.py::test_handle_completed_vertex_batch_computes_cost_usage_and_models` (a Vertex batch cost assertion), which is red on other open PRs against the same base and is unrelated to this change - [x] My PR's scope is as isolated as possible; it only solves 1 specific problem - [x] I have received a Greptile **Confidence Score of at least 4/5** before requesting a maintainer review ## Screenshots / Proof of Fix Real `/v1/responses` calls through a proxy I ran locally, against a live `gpt-5.6-sol` deployment, exporting to a self-hosted Langfuse. No mocks. Same request both times; only the checked-out revision of `langfuse_otel.py` differs. Shared setup — `config.yaml`: ```yaml model_list: - model_name: gpt-5.6-sol litellm_params: model: openai/gpt-5.6-sol api_base: http://<upstream-gateway>/v1 api_key: <redacted> model_info: mode: responses litellm_settings: callbacks: ["langfuse_otel"] ``` Env: `LANGFUSE_PUBLIC_KEY` / `LANGFUSE_SECRET_KEY` (redacted), `LANGFUSE_HOST=https://<self-hosted-langfuse>`. Request used in both runs: ```json {"model": "gpt-5.6-sol", "input": "Use the exec tool to print 6*7 in JavaScript.", "tools": [{"type": "custom", "name": "exec", "description": "Run JavaScript and return stdout"}], "tool_choice": "required", "store": false} ``` ### Before (`423b791`, this PR's merge base) 1. Send the request: ```console $ curl -s http://127.0.0.1:47311/v1/responses -H "authorization: Bearer $KEY" \ -H 'content-type: application/json' --data-binary @probe.json -w 'http=%{http_code}
' http=200 $ python -c "import json;d=json.load(open('resp.json'));print([i['type'] for i in d['output']])" ['custom_tool_call'] ``` 2. Read the generation back from Langfuse (`GET /api/public/observations/c67bd039acb8ec09`): ```json {"model": "gpt-5.6-sol", "input": [{"role": "user", "content": "Use the exec tool to print 6*7 in JavaScript."}], "output": null, "usageDetails": {"input": 67, "output": 18, "total": 85}} ``` The call succeeded and usage was recorded; the output is missing. ### After (`ae54fca`, this PR's tip) 1. Send the identical request: ```console $ curl -s http://127.0.0.1:47311/v1/responses -H "authorization: Bearer $KEY" \ -H 'content-type: application/json' --data-binary @probe.json -w 'http=%{http_code}
' http=200 $ python -c "import json;d=json.load(open('resp.json'));print([i['type'] for i in d['output']])" ['custom_tool_call'] ``` 2. Read the generation back from Langfuse (`GET /api/public/observations/b11b2f829cde8039`): ```json {"model": "gpt-5.6-sol", "input": [{"role": "user", "content": "Use the exec tool to print 6*7 in JavaScript."}], "output": [{"id": "h+YUGGZwtTX4S9/SRm/8djqt+UaFnjOYHLTCOBcIEqZ...", "name": "exec", "call_id": "call_C8wTc39sPHXh6ZpDvVQQ3vNn", "type": "custom_tool_call", "input": "console.log(6 * 7);
"}], "usageDetails": {"input": 67, "output": 18, "total": 85}} ``` Plain-text and `function_call` turns record their output in both runs — they were never affected. Unit test, red before the branch and green after, at `ae54fca`: ```console $ pytest tests/test_litellm/integrations/test_langfuse_otel.py -k responses_api # without the fix: 1 failed, 5 passed # with the fix: 6 passed ``` ## Type 🐛 Bug Fix ## Caveats (if any) - Only `/v1/responses` emits `custom_tool_call`; the other two endpoints are unaffected ### Final Attestation - [x] The tests check the right things, including the edge cases, and regressions in the respective real-world customer use-cases are not possible after this PR

## TLDR Problem this solves: - A Redis blip made `/key/update` return 400 after the write landed - Callers retried updates that had already been applied - Six other key endpoints carried the same latent 400 How it solves it: - Cache eviction after a committed write is now best-effort - Each of the two caches is guarded on its own - What could not be evicted is logged at error level ## User Flow Before: an admin whose gateway has a Redis outage cannot tell whether a key update applied 1. They send POST https://litellm-domain/key/update with `{"key": "sk-...", "max_budget": 42}` 2. They get back HTTP 400 and `"Authentication Error, Redis circuit breaker is open — skipping async_delete_cache"`, so their tooling records the update as failed 3. They send GET https://litellm-domain/key/info?key=sk-... and see `"max_budget": 42.0`, which contradicts the error they just got 4. With the two answers disagreeing, they retry the update and get another 400 for a change that was already in place After: the same request reports the success it is, and the operator still learns what went stale 1. They send POST https://litellm-domain/key/update with the same body 2. They get back HTTP 200 carrying the updated key with `"max_budget": 42.0` 3. GET https://litellm-domain/key/info?key=sk-... agrees, so there is nothing to retry 4. The proxy log carries an error line naming the key whose cached copy could not be dropped, saying it keeps its previous settings until that entry expires ## Relevant issues ## Linear ticket ## Pre-Submission checklist **Please complete all items before asking a LiteLLM maintainer to review your PR** - [x] I have added meaningful tests - [ ] My PR passes all CI/CD checks (e.g., lint, format, unit tests) - [x] My PR's scope is as isolated as possible; it only solves 1 specific problem - [ ] I have received a Greptile **Confidence Score of at least 4/5** before requesting a maintainer review (Greptile reviews automatically once the PR is opened; only comment `@greptileai` to re-request a review after pushing changes) ## Delays in PR merge? If you're seeing a delay in your PR being merged, ping the LiteLLM Team on [Slack (#pr-review)](https://join.slack.com/t/litellmossslack/shared_invite/zt-3o7nkuyfr-p_kbNJj8taRfXGgQI1~YyA). ## Screenshots / Proof of Fix Shared setup, a proxy pointed at a Redis that is not there: ```yaml model_list: - model_name: anthropic-sonnet-5 litellm_params: model: anthropic/claude-sonnet-5 api_key: os.environ/ANTHROPIC_API_KEY litellm_settings: cache: true cache_params: type: redis host: 127.0.0.1 port: 6390 password: "" ``` ```bash python litellm/proxy/proxy_cli.py --config repro_config.yaml --port 4001 ``` ### Before (d5b91b94d3) 1. Create a key to update ```bash curl -s -X POST "http://localhost:4001/key/generate" -H "Authorization: Bearer $MK" -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d '{"key_alias":"redis-evict-before","max_budget":100}' ``` ``` {"key":"sk-Sow0Cfl-s5rFN9MNKXSJFA", ... "max_budget":100 ...} ``` 2. Lower its budget to 42 ```bash curl -s -w "
HTTP %{http_code}
" -X POST "http://localhost:4001/key/update" -H "Authorization: Bearer $MK" -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d '{"key":"sk-Sow0Cfl-s5rFN9MNKXSJFA","max_budget":42}' ``` ``` {"error":{"message":"Authentication Error, Redis circuit breaker is open — skipping async_delete_cache","type":"auth_error","param":"None","code":"400"}} HTTP 400 ``` 3. Ask what the key actually holds now ```bash curl -s "http://localhost:4001/key/info?key=sk-Sow0Cfl-s5rFN9MNKXSJFA" -H "Authorization: Bearer $MK" ``` ``` max_budget = 42.0 ``` The 400 says the update failed, the key says it succeeded ### After (222307b1d2) 1. Create a key to update ```bash curl -s -X POST "http://localhost:4001/key/generate" -H "Authorization: Bearer $MK" -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d '{"key_alias":"redis-evict-after","max_budget":100}' ``` ``` {"key":"sk-RrsPqvmMGwwh7bbiRf4wRQ", ... "max_budget":100 ...} ``` 2. Lower its budget to 42, same unreachable Redis ```bash curl -s -w "
HTTP %{http_code}
" -X POST "http://localhost:4001/key/update" -H "Authorization: Bearer $MK" -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d '{"key":"sk-RrsPqvmMGwwh7bbiRf4wRQ","max_budget":42}' ``` ``` {"key": "sk-RrsPqvmMGwwh7bbiRf4wRQ", "key_alias": "redis-evict-after", "max_budget": 42.0} HTTP 200 ``` 3. Ask what the key actually holds now ```bash curl -s "http://localhost:4001/key/info?key=sk-RrsPqvmMGwwh7bbiRf4wRQ" -H "Authorization: Bearer $MK" ``` ``` max_budget = 42.0 ``` 4. Check what the proxy said about the cache it could not clear ```bash grep "Failed to evict cached key entry" proxy.log | tail -1 ``` ``` 12:07:08 - LiteLLM Proxy:ERROR: auth_checks.py:2381 - Failed to evict cached key entry f583f46fa2f51684680382e735ee1f068e42f3ab07d67e825cfdae8da3471ab2 from the internal usage cache; the key keeps authenticating with its previous settings until the entry's TTL expires: Redis circuit breaker is open — skipping async_delete_cache ``` ## Type 🐛 Bug Fix ## Caveats (if any) - The stale cached entry itself is unchanged, only the reported status - Retrying eviction in-request would not help while the breaker stays open - `/key/update` still never broadcasts eviction to peer workers ### Final Attestation - [x] The tests check the right things, including the edge cases, and regressions in the respective real-world customer use-cases are not possible after this PR

## TLDR Problem this solves: - `/anthropic/v1/files` 422s on OpenAI-only `purpose` instead of forwarding to Anthropic - Same route-shadowing bug #36092 fixed for `/openai_passthrough`, now on `/anthropic` How it solves it: - give `/anthropic` its own router, mounted ahead of the batches and files routers - every other provider prefix keeps today's behavior ## User Flow Before: a developer using the Anthropic SDK against the gateway cannot upload a document at all, so the model never sees their PDF 1. They set base URL `https://litellm-domain/anthropic` and send `POST https://litellm-domain/anthropic/v1/files` with `file=@plan.pdf`, what `client.beta.files.upload({ file })` emits 2. They get `422 {"detail":[{"type":"missing","loc":["body","purpose"],"msg":"Field required"}]}`, no file id, and nothing in their Anthropic account 3. With no file id they cannot reference the document in `POST https://litellm-domain/anthropic/v1/messages`, so the answer ignores their PDF After: the same upload reaches Anthropic instead of being intercepted locally 1. They set base URL `https://litellm-domain/anthropic` and send the same `POST https://litellm-domain/anthropic/v1/files` with `file=@plan.pdf`, no extra fields 2. The request reaches `api.anthropic.com`, so with a valid key they get back Anthropic's own file object and can reference its `file_id` in a later `/anthropic/v1/messages` call ## Relevant issues Fixes #37289 ## Linear ticket ## Pre-Submission checklist **Please complete all items before asking a LiteLLM maintainer to review your PR** - [x] I have added meaningful tests - [x] My PR passes all CI/CD checks (e.g., lint, format, unit tests) - [x] My PR's scope is as isolated as possible; it only solves 1 specific problem - [ ] I have received a Greptile **Confidence Score of at least 4/5** before requesting a maintainer review (Greptile reviews automatically once the PR is opened; only comment `@greptileai` to re-request a review after pushing changes) ## Delays in PR merge? If you're seeing a delay in your PR being merged, ping the LiteLLM Team on [Slack (#pr-review)](https://join.slack.com/t/litellmossslack/shared_invite/zt-3o7nkuyfr-p_kbNJj8taRfXGgQI1~YyA). ## Screenshots / Proof of Fix Config used for both runs: ```yaml model_list: - model_name: claude-haiku-4-5 litellm_params: model: anthropic/claude-haiku-4-5 api_key: os.environ/ANTHROPIC_API_KEY general_settings: master_key: sk-local-repro-master ``` Run with `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-invalid-key-for-local-repro` on purpose, same technique as the issue: a request LiteLLM answers itself fails 422 on `purpose`, a request that is forwarded to Anthropic fails 401 there instead. ### Before (41de13aa0c) 1. `POST /anthropic/v1/files`: ``` $ curl -s -w "
